  "id": "ENT056",
  "title": "Two Reversals Make a Return",
  "family": "fiction",
  "category": "mechanical puzzles",
  "premise": "A stage mechanic wants a pointer to turn clockwise, but two meshed toy gears reverse the input direction. They insert a third gear between input and pointer, turn the input clockwise, and watch the pointer rotate clockwise too, landing on a tiny applause sign.",
  "hook": "Why is the pointer going the wrong way?",
  "visible_action": "Two gears mesh and turn oppositely; a center idler slides into a new spaced layout; three gear arrows alternate directions.",
  "intended_takeaway_or_theme": "Count each reversal in a simple gear train.",
  "gate": "Verified external spur-gear direction parity: two meshes preserve input direction. Gear centers, compatible tooth pitch, and clearances must be consistent; this is a simplified gear-train model, not a fabrication guide.",
